<!-- ============ SPOTLIGHT — WONDER ============ -->
<section class="dept" id="spotlight" data-dept="spotlight">
  <h3 class="dept-name">🗽 Spotlight Feature</h3>
  <p class="dept-sub">One story a day gets the cover treatment. Today, she's 140.</p>
  <article class="spotlight-body">
    <h4>The Lady in the Harbor</h4>
    <p>She was late, she arrived in pieces, and nobody was sure the money
    would hold out to put her together. The Statue of Liberty — sculptor
    <b>Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi's</b> copper giant, her skin stretched over
    an iron frame engineered by <b>Gustave Eiffel</b> — crossed the Atlantic
    in <b>214 crates</b> aboard the French ship <i>Isère</i> in 1885, a gift
    from the people of France that America then had to raise the money to
    stand up.</p>
    <p>On <b>October 28, 1886 — 140 years ago today</b> — President Grover
    Cleveland dedicated her on Bedloe's Island in New York Harbor. She stands
    <b>305 feet from the ground to the tip of the torch</b>. The copper started
    the color of a new penny; the sea air spent forty years turning her the
    green the whole world now knows.</p>
    <p>Three years before she sailed, a poet named <b>Emma Lazarus</b> wrote
    her a sonnet for a fundraiser — <i>The New Colossus</i> — and gave the
    statue her voice: <i>"Give me your tired, your poor, your huddled masses
    yearning to breathe free."</i> The plaque went up inside the pedestal in
    1903, and the words outlived everyone who argued about them.</p>
    <p class="desk-line"><b>Why she leads today:</b> she turns 140 on our
    birthday — Dawnfold's first edition — and she is still the first thing
    America says to anyone arriving by water. Some covers choose themselves.</p>
    <p class="src">Dedication date and President Cleveland: Wikipedia, "October 28." Photograph: public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.</p>
  </article>
</section>

